d.paper.practicalImplications="Use the average of CMJ trials performed without arm swing to track neuromuscular status, since average height was more sensitive than highest height in this review. Choose monitoring variables with a small-to-moderate CV and a moderate-to-large effect size, as the authors recommend, and treat fatigue signals with caution because the average-value fatigue evidence is limited.";
d.paper.athleteDev="Use the average of several CMJ trials, without arm swing, to track fatigue and supercompensation, and prefer variables with a small-to-moderate CV and a moderate-to-large effect size. Jump height is simple to take from flight time, but the number of trials to average is not established and the average-value fatigue evidence is thin.";
save("Q48.json",d);
d=load("Q42.json");
d.paper.findings=d.paper.findings.replace("18 used rolling averages, five used the exponentially weighted moving average (EWMA), and four compared both.","Eighteen studies used rolling averages alone and four compared rolling averages with the exponentially weighted moving average (EWMA); the authors also refer to five EWMA studies, a count the text does not reconcile.");
save("Q42.json",d);
d=load("Q44.json");
d.paper.tldr=d.paper.tldr.replace("but peak power was too variable to use for monitoring.","but peak power was too variable (CV 40 to 58%) to rely on for monitoring.");
d.paper.practicalImplications=d.paper.practicalImplications.replace("Do not base decisions on peak power, whose variability was very high, and judge changes against the typical error and SWC, since most usefulness ratios were below 1.","Peak power showed very high variability (CV 39.8 to 57.8%; ICC 0.11 to 0.30), so these data do not support using it for monitoring. Judge changes against the typical error and SWC, since most usefulness ratios were below 1.");
d.paper.athleteDev=d.paper.athleteDev.replace("Do not use peak power as a monitoring metric, and interpret changes against the typical error before acting on them.","Peak power's very high variability (CV 40 to 58%) means these data do not support it as a monitoring metric, and changes should be read against the typical error.");
d.populations=["Male Athletes","Healthy Athletes","Youth / Adolescent","Professional / Elite"];
save("Q44.json",d);
console.log("ok");
' && cd .. && node helpers/g6/check.js && grep -c "18 used rolling\|do not base\|Do not use peak" drafts/Q42.json drafts/Q44.json drafts/Q48.json

